#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078179 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078421 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "839431c8-b292-5ed9-b549-2d81b12df45b" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying an Arithmetic Sequence"}] :question/id #uuid "eba75ca0-7f78-531f-96c4-b772b5785828" :question/math-academy-id "e-14375" :question/problem "Which of the three sequences below are arithmetic sequences?\n\n1. $2, \\: 4, \\: 6, \\: 8, \\: \\ldots$\n2. $5, \\: 2, \\: -1, \\: -4, \\: \\ldots$\n3. $2, \\: 5, \\: 8, \\: 12, \\: \\ldots$" :question/worked-solution "To determine whether each sequence is an arithmetic sequence, we need to look at the jumps between terms in the sequence. If the jumps are the same, then they indeed represent a common difference of an arithmetic sequence.\n\n- In sequence I, the jumps are all the same. Therefore, sequence I is an arithmetic sequence with common difference $d=2$.\n\n![](images/17/17933a1cabd8d487dd1607f7ae7064d6e186e3454a52497977f866150c491f25.png)\n\n- In sequence II, the jumps are all the same. Therefore, sequence II is an arithmetic sequence with common difference $d=-3$.\n\n![](images/9d/9d9a41035215deb27d0578123a859d9b492b505d79b0a0a71f62c822942298ca.png)\n\n- In sequence III, the jumps are not all the same. Therefore, sequence III is not an arithmetic sequence.\n\n![](images/2b/2b860338be6ff8876c1368ed5f08162096873fa9992a9e88d7ff545752a5bd36.png)\n\nIn conclusion, only sequences I and II are arithmetic sequences."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078180 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078422 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "73efb24d-e21f-5999-b545-2e3539440735" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Terms of an Arithmetic Sequence"}] :question/id #uuid "510e7581-c8f5-51b0-931b-02f6e926e5f2" :question/math-academy-id "e-14381" :question/problem "What is the $8$th term of the following arithmetic sequence?\n$4, \\: 7, \\: 10, \\: 13, \\: 16, \\: \\ldots$" :question/worked-solution "![](images/ac/ac878a3d258fa19641d983863580af953bbea593b49bffff16f2f09eb7b6ab2d.png)\n\nWe notice that the sequence is an arithmetic sequence with a common difference of\n$d=7-4=3$.\n\nSo, we can calculate each term in the sequence by adding $3$ to the previous term.\n\nWe will continue listing terms of the sequence until we get to the $8$th term. Listing the terms up to the $8$th term, we have:\n\n![](images/e5/e536739b28db7d0f221967ce8729eee502a29f80caec8e1c39f61a9cfe4a6459.png)\n\nTherefore, the $8$th term is $25$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078194 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078420 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ac161f82-668f-5e67-b8d8-c2f6a438c7a8" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Common Difference"}] :question/id #uuid "ae7753b6-b58d-57c5-849e-c887e7d1601e" :question/math-academy-id "e-14731" :question/problem "What is the common difference of the following arithmetic sequence?\n$7,\\: 2,\\: -3,\\: -8,\\: \\ldots$" :question/worked-solution "The common difference $d$ of an arithmetic sequence is the difference between any two consecutive terms.\n\nTherefore, the common difference for our sequence is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nd &= a_{2} - a_{1} \\\\\n&= 2 - 7 \\\\\n&=-5\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078883 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078421 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "839431c8-b292-5ed9-b549-2d81b12df45b" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying an Arithmetic Sequence"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ae56415c-614e-5010-84b2-152c6dbf8e7d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186076077} {:answer/id #uuid "0007cb31-26a9-5baa-bb56-3315e50f92d7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186076078} {:answer/id #uuid "5423d064-430d-5df3-844e-c5b95a86e210" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186076079} {:answer/id #uuid "573eb60d-c9a8-5e53-86cf-c5b203b832ab" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186076080} {:answer/id #uuid "57aa2166-1627-5c3f-b2d3-eb4cdfbc2479" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186076081}] :answer-field/id #uuid "7ddb97aa-753c-55ab-a316-b6f60bf556c5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186076076}] :question/id #uuid "d14404fc-deb2-5ffc-b0b6-821e376bc0d3" :question/math-academy-id "q-39107" :question/problem "Which of the three sequences below are arithmetic sequences?\n\n1. $6, 10, 14, 18,..$.\n2. $4, 8, 16, 32,..$.\n3. $87, 74, 61, 46,..$."}]}
